A||||6||JohnnyK||Slave cylinder questions||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||04-26-2001||06:37 PM||||Well, still trying to troubleshoot the clutch. Rebuilt master in front, new line, now, that leaves the slave.. I have a question (I just finished bleeding it, so I don't know if the master was the problem.. Hasn't failed again yet.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> When I undo the bleeder screw on the slave (5 speed), even just like, 1/8th of a turn, the screw it really loose (can rock it back and forth) and it seems fluid comes out the threads. But when you seal it back up, it's tight and good. Is there any problem, or are they all like this? THanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||baldlobo||04-26-2001||06:42 PM||||have you tried locktight?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tryxalon||04-26-2001||09:07 PM||||IF your slave cylinder is one of the plastic bodied ones - give up and buy a new slave (not a rebuilt). <P>Sorry, but you will NEVER get that bleeder to work right once you "overtorque" it. <P>(I went through 3 of them trying!!)<P>(I also wound up re-engineering the piston in that plastic slave cylinder after I found out the volume of my slave was .6 cubic inches larger than the Master - but that's a different story!) <P><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 04-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||04-26-2001||11:07 PM||||Tried locktight? No man, I'm just wondering if everyones does that, or are they supposed to really snug until you take the out all the way. My brake ones aren't like that one. But that one doesn't seem to leak? So I dunno.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-26-2001||11:23 PM||||Without seeing it it's hard to say.<P>(I would not locktite a bleeder. ever. they break easy enough as it is...)<P>As long as it seals and stays closed.... Sort of up to you...It might hold but I wouldn't trust it.<P>Assuming you didn't wreck the threads... One posibility is you've got a bleeder with the wrong threads. On smaller sizes it can be easy to mix metric and SAE. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jimmy||04-27-2001||12:20 AM||||I have to buy a new slave tomorow as my brother snapped the whole bleeder assembly off mine(slave was in bad condition, rusty).  Do not locktight the bleeder screw unless you plan on never bleeding it again.<BR>Good Luck<P>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000006||LivoniaFieroGT||04-27-2001||05:16 PM||||Had similar problem a couple of years back... just replace slave.  The threads on the bleeder nut or slave are stretched.  The new one installed would turn out 1 3/4 times before fluid bled.  Don't locktite.<P>||||reg||1
